ning , due to other rl activities, I willbe typing my handwritten ;) point list in electronic form tomorrow.Most important thing is the way i wanna structure the work we need to do,but its too long to type in right now so I really have to do it tomorrow.See yaGaladriel**hmm, I forgot to say: If you wanna discuss things without me, fine, butdon't start making decisions, deviding jobs, or doing work on it b4 you have seen the list of points i'll bring. ;*)G.**i have put a file in /pl/galadriel/doc/questdoc1its not finished but its something to start looking atG**======================= Galadriel, 27 aug 1993 =========================This document is a summary of some brainstorming about the quests.It is not a plan!It is not my own opinion!It _is_ just a summary of all things that I could think of andthat _could_ be useful in the discussion about the future of the quests.========================================================================current problems:=================Some quests are:- bugged.- badly coded.- too easy.- the solutions are known.possible solutions to current problems:=======================================- IF: bugged...+ let the owner wiz debug the area+ if it is imported or the wiz is gone, let somene else debug it.+ remove the quest.+ remove the area completely.- IF: badly coded...+ let the owner wiz update the area+ if it is imported or the wiz is gone, let somene else update it.+ remove the quest.+ remove the area completely.- IF: too easy...+ make it harder.+ reduce the quest points.+ remove the quest.+ remove the area completely.- IF: the solutions are known...+ change the quest to have a different solution.+ change the quest so it doesnt have always the same solution.+ remove the quest.+ remove the area completely.possible new problems caused by a solution:===========================================+ IF: let the owner wiz debug the area...  OR: let the owner wiz update the area...- the owner is not gone entirely, but just not on often, on holidays,  very busy in real life etc.+ IF: it is imported or the wiz is gone, let somene else debug it...  OR: it is imported or the wiz is gone, let somene else update it...- Nobody has time to do it.- Nobody wants to do it.- Its always the same who have to do it.+ IF: remove the quest...- players liked the quest and will not like it that it is gone.- if a quest is removed, the %rating of players that did the quest will  drop but the %rating of the other players will increase. This is not fair.- The increase of %rating of players that didnt do the rm'ed quest could  be big enough to lift them to the needed %rating to wiz!- there are so few quests left, that the time to do the needed %rating is  too short and too many players will wiz too fast.+ IF: remove the area...- it is a big area and HM will be much smaller afterwards.- players liked the area and will not like it that it is gone.+ IF: make it harder...- The players who _want to do it_ don't like it.+ IF: reduce the quest points...- The players who _did it already_ don't like it.- The total amouth of questpoints in HM goes down so people who did it  go down but people who didn't do it go up in %rating+ IF: change the quest to have a different solution...  OR: change the quest so it doesnt have always the same solution...- The wiz doesnt want it changed.- The wiz doesnt have the time.- This quest can simply not have different solution.Some points on unhappy players:===============================- Players can be very upset about things. They can complain about very small  and virtually unimportant matters and also they can complain about things  that are really important.- Many times players are not mad about the change itself, but rather the  way they feel treated by wizards.- Players don't like big changes.- Players don't like unexpectant changes.- Players don't like unexplained changes.Some points on unhappy wizzes:==============================- Wizzes are often proud of the areas and quests they created. If their  quest is disconnected they will usually not be happy.- Wizzes can talk and think for themselves. First thing should be to  explain to the wiz that his quest needs to be changed or removed,  and then ask the wiz if (s)he agrees on that, instead of just doing  the removal and telling the wiz afterwards.- Wizzes get annoyed if you rm their code, better disconnect it only  and give them the possibility to backup the code.- If the wiz doesnt agree, try to find out why. (S)he could have a good  story, instead of just being stubborn. So always _listen_ to her/him.- Most wizzes are sensible enough to cooperate. There could be reached  an arrangement to change the quest instead of removing it.- There could be the arrangement that the wiz codes a new better quest  instead of this one.Some wild idea's:=================+ Players don't loose the points of a removed quest.+ Players that loose a set quest get another quest set without doing it.+ Players get a dummy quest with the points of the removed quest.+ Players that did a quest thats will be removed, recieve something else  instead of the questpoints they loose. example: A autoload badge,  an item that will help a bit while questing or has some other nice  feature.+ Dont change,add,remove more than one quest in 3 weeks.  . This way people will have time to get used to new %ratings.  . The %ratings dont change spectacularly, causing an outburst of anger    or tens of new wizzes.+ Don't decrease the number of quests. So only remove an old quest if a  new one is installed. Of course the number can increase as more wizzes  code quests.+ Don't change the total number of quest points in HM. So a quest can be  removed if other with the same total amount get installed.**I figured out how Q's quest works with the character locks. Why notuse them to code quests. Sure it'll be more work and it would takequite a bit more coding but if you cannot bother to make a goodquest then the quest shouldn't be coded at all. I think you shouldalso rotate the quests around. I agree that some quests are weighedtoo heavily. Who gives a damn about easy quests like pardone's, orc slayer, and the like? I'm not exactly sure but at least one ofthe easy quests is worth too much in my opinion.I'm working on Alex's area and recoding everything. It's going tobe a good quest I know. If anyone could help out, check out/players/colossus/room.c It's a room environment I'm trying tomake to make his quest better. Too many repititious rooms withoutanything special makes it boring.Colossus**I read Kbl's note on quest continuation. Maybe it could be possible toarrange a gathering of all wizards concerning coding future quests andmaybe make them coincide and link them. For example:Destroy Lolth the evil spider queen and to do that you have to:1. Create the famous Drow Slayer (half of the quests)2. To actually kill Lolth (another half of the quest)Just a suggestionColossus**First i wanted to start with Re: questdoc1 of GaladrielA) I think when a quest or area is imported it shouldn't stay on the HolyMission. This mud isn't supposed to be a copy of many muds.I think you should code creative or don't code at all.B) I don't think it is necessary to remove a quest completly(with some exceptions). When a quest of an area is removed (temp) oftenan area can still be a normal nice area with some changes. Not onewizard has more than one quest on the possible removal list. So theycould find time to change it in the way it is possible to place the quest back in time.One remark about the exceptions I mentioned before: There's onecertain area where not one wiz is happy about. He's a big memory eaterand left bad feeling wiht a lot of ppl. His 2 quests  should be removed_and_ (cause the area contains some important things) his area should betaken over by anohter wiz who 1) cares about the monks 2) sure will changethis area in a normal _non_ sexual area. But that is not the question now.C) When a guest is too easy:A quest is never to easy or to hard. Ther must be quests in every categorySo a level 2 player must be able to find a quest he can solve. The onlything that canbe wrong is that the given quest points don't match the level of the quest.D) if the solutions are known:This kind of quests should be (temporarly) removed. With these quests the players just follow the information told by others. Togheter with the coden there should be decided if the area is changed to a non quest area or if the quest gets serious changes.You shouldn't remove a quest for ever. I think the best solution on thisquest matter is to let the quests rolate. (change from time to time)2] CATEGORIESwith 33 quests you could easily make groups of 5 quests :very easy quests -> \"E\" questseasy quests -> \"D\" questsa bit hard quests -> \"C\" questshard quests -> \"B\" questsvery hard quests -> \"A\" questsYou should solve 4 of the 5 quests of each category.With a log file you could count how many players have solved the quest. When par exemple 10 or 15 players solved the quest it could be replaced by a questof the same category. This way you also don't have the problem withangry wizards. There are not 'real' removals. The 'removed' quests areplaced in a 'waitinglist'. So the area is still open and players can stillsolve the quest but _bad luck_, if they are not smart enought to read the list and follow the instructions told by others, they don't get any points.When a new quests is coded some wizards should play it and decide in whichcategory it belongs. Then it is added immediatly and the oldestor most solved quest is placed in the waitinglist. This wayit can take some time b 4 an old quest reenters. This also gives the codersof the quests in the waitinglist time to update or improve it.3] % RATINGWith this new system there should be a way to change it all without making thede\\increase more than 20%Then there will not be in their list or score: You have a rating of x%.But: YOu need 4 quests in each category. YOu have now:x A, x B, x C ect ectWith this system (which i would love to code but I'm not good enough for)will not give problems when there comes in a new quest.When a player solved a certain number of a certain category that numberwill not increase when the quest disappears from the list to the waitinglist. This way there will be no unhappy players in the future:They get various quests and can choose often.Then there is only the problem left wiht the %rating when the switch is made.I think it is possible to count like following: OLD % RATING            NEW SYSTEM0-5                     1 quest6-9 %                       210-13                       314-17                       418-21                       522-25                       626-29                       7ect ect 72-75                       1876-79                       1980-83                       20Ect ectThis way the old 80 % will be equal to 20 quests which equals 80% of25 quests.There should be some discussions how these quest are shared among the different categoriesWhen the waitinglist gets too long we could increase the number of questsin each categories and the amount of quests to be solved (but this wouldn't come up untill there would be more than 50 quests)4] 	LIST - WAITINGLISTThis last chapter will need a lot of discussion ( if u all like the plan)I made the following possible list:Cat A (very hard) 1 Free king dimitri                   2 Destroy HaunterCat B (hard)     1 quench evil hellfire                 2 make sad girl happy                 3 help elfcityCat C            a vampire quest                 2 retrieve black orbCat D            1 make dinner for druid lakmir                  2 abby of the roseCat E            1 walking castleThese categories I choose on own experienceFor the rest I had in mind:(could be placed on waitinglist)Save holy mission Cat Eget orcslayer Cat EGoblinquest Cad DTrollslayer Cad D or ERatsquest Cad DKings head Cat CGanja seeds Cad Dcerberos victim Cad D or Cchaosblade of death cat bearth quest Cat EThe rest of the quest (7 left) I didn't do so I couldn'tjudge their categories.The quest not include are:Ask_god it is the only compulsory quest there will beMill_quest and witchquest see chapter 1Btotal newbie quest maybe the newbies could use money or experience moreAs last remark I din't want to insult any of the wizards. If I did i offer thatwizard my apoligize.Greeting ,Lovely Berenice**I have done most of the quests on this MUD, with the exception of Gnome King,Island dragon, AnySlayer, Viking and Cure Dwarf.  Let me comment on theremaining quests, in descending order of difficulty:1) Haunter of the Dark:  excellent quest, many difficult puzzles, a bit of hack'n slash in the beginning.  This quest would be ideal if there were a fewmore clues as to how to start it - right now, just FINDING the areais unlikely.  There's a note in the well in the mountains of madness - thatnote (and well) should perhaps be moved down into the moors somewhere (nearthe farmhouse, for instance?)4) Vampire king: this and elfcity are the last remnants of the old HM\"hack & slash\" style quests.  With the effective removal of stonefish fangs,this quest is extremely difficult and time-consuming for certain characterclasses.  As it offers very little in the way of puzzles to solve, and asthe solution is widely known, I would suggest that its rating and the monstersincorporated in it be both greatly reduced.5) Orb quest: this has better clues, much less fighting, and a bit moreproblem-solving than the vampire quest.  As far as I know, it is rarelysolved, and I have never seen a mailnote with its solution.  I would keepit as it is.6)  Hellfire: this quest is also widely known, and the puzzles are prettystraightforward.  The baal-rog, however, is a real player-killer, as itsroom does not permit wimpying.  I would suggest that the baal-rog's strengthbe halved, the room modified to permit wimpy to work (players die of lag, whichis not their fault), and the quest % should be reduced.7) Elfcity:  This quest is also based on hack 'n slash, with the sea drake andthe evil elf (who now appears to follow players) being the two bad examples.Danahara is almost unkillable, and her room doesn't permit wimpy- however,there is a way around this problem which is not documented in any mailnoteI've seen.  I would keep this quest at its current rating, but degrade thestrength of the evil elf and the sea drake to perhaps 1/3 their currentstrength10) Dimitri: again, Moonchild has written a fine quest which has too fewclues about how to begin.  Sleeping in the tree should be more specific aboutwhat is needed, and perhaps a hint about magic snakes living far undergroundwould be appropriate.11) Make girl happy: this quest's solution is widely known, its puzzles arenot terribly intuitive, and the \"drop weapons\" requirement is a killer forthe Ninjas (who lose their autoloading weapons if dropped).  It should bemodified and the no-weapon requirement should be removed.13) Abbey: I don't know why this is rated so high.  There is essentially onlyONE puzzle in the whole quest, and its solution (the burnished door) iswidely known.  It should be changed or degraded (%) or both.14)  Quest perilous: I actually liked this quest quite a bit - it had somekilling, some puzzle-solving, and the clues were sufficient to finish thequest without giving it away.  I would leave it as-is.15)  Good King's head: the solution to this is known, though not widely,and the mutating maze is very hard unless you know the direction sequenceto get through it.  Other than that, a good quest.16)  Evil troll king: No clues as to how to start this one either (are youlistening, Moonchild??).  Other than that, straight hack 'n slash, with thetrollking being particularly vicious.  I'd lower the fighting strength ofthe trollking and the 3 trolls slightly, and change the beginning part tobe a little more involved and intuitive than drinking from a poison stream.17)  Chaosdemon: excellent quest, silver dragon shouldn't be so hard.  Solutionis WIDELY known as this quest appears on other MUDs.  Depending on how oftenthis one gets solved, it could be left, degraded or eliminated.18)  Hermit: straight hack 'n slash, complicated now by the fact that Mooniehas moved the hermit.  I would reduce the number of goblin scalps to 10 and/orkeep them from wimpying (this is REALLY annoying on this MUD, as wimpyingmonsters get the first attack when you chase them - bug?).19)  Lakmir quest: good quest, nice trick with the rug.  No changes.20)  Evil time maze: put the hut in more accessable places (who will ever findit up in Moonchild's frostgiant wastelands??), otherwise a fine questfor those whole like mapping mazes.21)  Angmar's treasury: also a great quest, also widely known (sigh), samesuggestions as Chaosdemon.22)  Ganja harvest:  solution is known, Herp's puzzle makes it a bit harder.I'd leave it as is or uprate the % a bit.23)  Help little girl: fairly unintuitive and many ways to go wrong.  Solutionalso widely known.  Witch is a pain because she's always being killed for exp.Suggest that her exp be lowered to 400 or so to eliminate that.  Also, syntaxis funny at times (polishing the iron key pops to mind)24)  Abduluh's heart: good quest, Denizen shouldn't be so hard for high-levelplayers (howinhell is a 25th level figher supposed to kill a 50th levelmonster unaided??).  Maybe make monster_level = 1.5 * playerlevel if level<10,otherwise 1.2* level.  This is supposed to be an easy quest, remember...25)  Solar quest: not too bad, widely known solution.  Since it's worth solittle, leave it as-is.26)  Padrone's castle: Also widely known, but a good beginner quest.  Leaveas-is.27)  Cerberos: known MUD-wide, and dragon is too hard to kill for a questworth this little.  Eliminate or change.28)  Save holy mission:  cute quest, but requires a lot of MUD-knowledge fora quest of this level.  Put the translater in the newbie tree in chantilly'sgarden or something.  Solution is also known MUD-wide.29)  Free man:  solution is not known mud-wide, and although the flow ofthis quest is quite linear, the puzzles are somewhat challenging.  Raise the% for this one a bit.30)  Yorel: very odd syntax for start/end of quest, and rats can get to beoverwhelming when there are lots of them.  Keep them from wimpying, make yorelmore responsive, keep quest % or raise it a bit.31)  Hobbe: good quest - yes, it's quite easy, but it will be one of thefirst quests anyone will do and it's a good intro to questing.  Leave as-is.32)  Newbie area:  leave as-is.  Good intro.33)  Orc slayer: eliminate.I've probably stepped on quite a few toes with the comments.  I mean nooneany affront (please don't take it out on my little mortal body :).  Theseare just how I see the quests, based on my experience with them and talkingwith other players.Yours,  Cbr**I notice that the comunication on this board is pretty slow.For example I would like to have some remarks on my note 10so I can work things out further or think on other solutions.This way it's gonna take forever.                lovely Berenice**I changed Dmitri as the solution to the puzzle was too well known.I changed Troll Slayer to make the beginning more logical.Moonchild**Good! I'm changing my fairy area in an area where there is a quest and a area which don't has a quest. I only have to change a few rooms or monsters and I have the quesst in.Berenice**  As things now stand, it is very hard to become a wizard on this MUD.Consider my case: dispite solving the hardest quest here, plus doingmost of the rest of them, I still need another 8% to reach the requisite80% figure.  That means I'll have to solve the Gnome King quest, and perhapsone other as well.  IMHO, that is too much to ask.  I hear all the wizardscomplaining about quest info sharing between players, but then you presentthose players with a scenario in which the only reasonable way to becomea wizard is to colloborate illegally with other players.  It should comeas no surprise that they do so!  Part of the problem is that quests are being added at a furious rate.  Whileobviously wizards want to make themselves a quest and have players solveit, it places an undue burden on the players since each quest which is added drops their quest % a certain amount; the harder the quest, thegreater the drop.  I have *barely* kept even at >70% over the past 6weeks, dispite solving Haunter and now Viking, not to mention goingthrough the danger of completing the Vampire quest.  It is depressingto be working so hard while not making any real progress.ThereforeSuggestion:  Change the quest system such that, for each new quest which gets added,the quest % required to reach any given level goes down a correspondingamount - that is, make it so that it takes the same level of quest-solvingto reach any given level (including wizard) regardless of how many newquests are added.  This means that the %s will have to be altered eachtime a quest is added, removed or revalued.Note that, while this will not hurt players when quests are added, it willdisadvantage those who have done a quest which is subsequently devaluedor removed.  My suggestion in those cases is that those players not beinjured by the devaluation or removal of such a quest - they should begiven credit (with a dummy quest, or whatever is appropriate) so thattheir % does not change as a result of the quest change.Comments?  Reactions?  Flames?Your (currently) mortal friend,  Cbr**Hi, short comment,Think the changes notified here are good ones, its a tiny start but weare at least moving now.About the suggestions of Cbr:I wanted to save my ideas and opinion a bit coz I wanna be on a sort ofoverseeing neutrally position for a while. Still I want already to saythat these points I though also at first but the problem is that this wayit will become easier to wiz when more quests are added. Its obvious:The % stays same but there is more choice which quests to do. So you canswitch quest more easily if you cant figure out this one there are moreto choose from.What could be a solution? There are many, some already in my starting doc.Having a maximum number of changes a month as example would allow players towiz if he works hard for it. Another would be to drop the needed % each timebut not as much as Cbr suggests etc , there are more ways..Will post a more complete doc again later. See ya, keep brainstorming ;)Galadriel for Holy Mission.**  As Galadriel points out, my changes would make it somewhat easier to completeenough quests to wiz because players would have more choice in whatquests they did - there would be more ways to fulfill the quest requirement.On the other hand, this MUD is arguably very hard to wiz on right now -there hasn't been a new wizard since (as I recall) late June or early July.Making things somewhat easier would not be such a bad thing, IMO.As things now stand, the system punishes people who don't have as much timeto play (and thus get left behind in quest points) as well as thosewho don't cheat.  I still think that keeping the quest requirementsfixed with regards to new quests is a better system than what we currentlyhave.  My quest % has dropped again, too.  Now I'll need to do Gnome AND some other quest to wiz, dispite being the only one to solve Haunter,the first to do Viking, and having done all of the \"old\" quests exceptfor Elfcity.  *grumble*Cbr**I have reduced %age for wiz to 75.Would arches please check when they approve quests that they are not asteaming pile of horse manure, unlike some of the quests that have beenapproved in the last 6 months?Moonie**  We haven't had a note in almost 2 weeks, and I feelobligated to post one.  A few comments which I have picked up...1) Killing the denizen in Sarge's quest is outrageously hard for higher   level characters, since the denizen is automatically twice their    level.  Since this quest is supposed to be easy, I really think this    should be changed.  I've had a bunch of complaints (and at least one death  of a 27th level char) about him.2) With Moonchild gone, someone should take over his quests.  In particular,   Haunter virtually requires a player to be able to teleport in order   to finish in a timely fashion, and this places an (IMHO) undue burden   on non-mages.  With the loss of the usual teleport items (witch ball   and so forth), I think the quest should be modified to remove that    requirement.  I can discuss the exact change with whoever will be  modifying it.On a personal note, I know a lot of you have been asking me to wiz.  Believe me, I am considering it daily.  It is not at all an easy decision,and there are good arguments to be made on both sides of the issue.Yours,  Cbr**did all our brains stop storming?has this board become obsolete?if its coz HM is temp closed, so be it.if its coz we are tired of improving HM, sad.but if its coz we're just out of idea's, tell me coz then ill posta summary of what we have now and then we can start doing things.Galadriel for Holy Mission.**	Well It no longer exists in a loding sense. His area was Archivedbecause the only reason a player went there was for the quest.So I decided that it was a waste of obj loading to have it.So thats one less quest to think about..		Warlord**"
